  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Let \\(n\\) be the number of pairs of values of \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) such that \\(3x+by+c=0\\) and \\(cx-2y+12=0\\) have the same graph. Then \\(n\\) is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "finite but more than \\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "greater than any finite number"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": "2"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1964-p18.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "For the same graph, \\((c,-2,12)=k(3,b,c).\\) Thus \\(c=3k\\) and \\(12=kc=3k^2,\\) so \\(k=\\pm2.\\) Each value determines one pair through \\(b=-\\frac{2}{k}\\) and \\(c=3k.\\) Hence there are two pairs.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Coincident line equations have proportional coefficient triples",
        "Set \\((c,-2,12)=k(3,b,c)\\) and use the first and third coordinates"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
